# The Azure App Service host needs these settings, configured in the portal
# under the web app's Settings -> Environment variables (not in this file, and
# not in the repo). SQL_SERVER and SQL_DATABASE are addressing only:
# authentication to the database uses the app's managed identity, so there is
# no database password anywhere.
#
#   SQL_SERVER=sql-icebrk-test.database.windows.net
#   SQL_DATABASE=icebrk-test
#
# SESSION_SECRET signs the sign-in cookie. Use 32+ random characters, e.g. the
# output of `openssl rand -base64 48`. Until it is set, server.js keeps sign-in
# off and everyone plays as a guest. Changing it signs every host out.
#
#   SESSION_SECRET=<random string>
#
# To run the API locally against the real database, sign in with `az login`
# first — server.js uses azure-active-directory-default, which picks up your
# Azure CLI session on your machine and the managed identity in Azure.
#
#   az login
#   npm run build && SQL_SERVER=... SQL_DATABASE=... SESSION_SECRET=... npm start
#
# Front-end only work needs none of this: `npm run dev` runs the SPA with
# session-local state, exactly as a signed-out guest sees it.
